## Exploring new functions through computational de novo protein design
The advent of computational protein design has been made possible by a combination of advances in computational power, gene synthesis technologies, protein structure prediction and computational algorithms [29,33,36]. Rosetta is currently one of the leading software suites for protein modeling and design [36]. Since initially developed more than two decades ago, it has continuously been improved by the scientific community, and new methods have been instrumental in enabling the rapid evolution of the de novo proteins field. The first applications of computational protein design focused on redesigning natural proteins to solve a variety of challenges in biomedicine and synthetic biology [37,38]. Some past and recent examples are the stabilization of proteins [39,40] for the development of more effective vaccines against malaria [41], enhanced enzymes to treat celiac disease [42], self-assembling nanoparticles for the presentation of immunogenic epitopes [43], and the optimization of numerous antibodies [44e48]. Nevertheless, such approaches rely on the use of natural proteins as starting points, which limits the overall scope to the improvement (or adaptation) of pre-existing natural protein structures (and functions).
Eventually, computational protein design methods advanced to a point where it became possible to create fully de novo proteins, free of the constraints imposed by evolution into natural proteins [29]. Initial efforts in de novo design focused on establishing the basic design principles that allow the systematic generation of de novo proteins [36,49e51]. The great effort invested in early research paid off, not only by producing proteins with new folds but also by delivering novel proteins with remarkable stability [49]. Ultimately, the first examples of de novo designed proteins demonstrated that computational protein design could be applied to accurately engineer entirely new proteins with biochemical and biophysical properties that (often) exceed those of naturally occurring proteins. Nevertheless, none of these de novo proteins were originally designed for (or capable of) accomplishing a biologically relevant function. Since then, a large number of de novo “protein scaffolds” (i.e., well-folded protein structures without function) spanning a wide range of shapes and sizes have been designed (and confirmed experimentally) [52e60]. Such a diverse repertoire of novel hyperstable proteins also has served as a starting point for the development of functional molecules. For instance, introducing (functional) motifs into inert de novo protein scaffolds (known as motif grafting) has allowed protein designers to create several functional de novo proteins [61,62]. Recent studies have demonstrated that the motif grafting approach and its variations are widely applicable. Some examples include the creation of thousands of high-affinity mini-proteins with therapeutic potential against influenza and botulinum neurotoxin (Figure 2a) [63] and bioactive protein switches with customizable cellular functions (Figure 2d) [64].
A second, more challenging protein design approach, is creating functional de novo proteins that are tailored from their conception to achieve a desired function. Simultaneously designing a protein’s fold and its function can be more challenging than adapting an existing protein scaffold. Such a “purposefully-functional” de novo protein design requires a high level of control over the shape, size, and function of the final designs (as well as prior knowledge or assumptions about the structure of the targeted functional site). Thus, the de novo design of functional proteins remained an elusive landmark in the field. The earliest successes of this design paradigm include the design of an EpsteineBarr viral protein inhibitor [65], a designed scaffold for stabilizing a neutralizing epitope of the respiratory syncytial virus [66], and a transmembrane $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ -transporting helical protein [67]. More recently, designers have achieved the development of pH-sensitive protein oligomers (Figure 2c) [68]; a de novo designed fluorescent protein (Figure 2b) [69]; and Neo-2/15, a de novo protein with biologically-relevant immunomodulatory function (Figure 2e) [35]. Altogether, these examples illustrate how advances in de novo computational protein design are enabling the creation of novel functional proteins with potential applications in a wide range of scientific and technological fields.
## De novo designed proteins can exhibit exceptional robustness
Natural protein evolution, in most cases, prioritizes protein function over stability [25,70]. As a result, most natural proteins have structural irregularities, which translate to nonideal properties that pose challenges for the development of therapeutic applications, such as low solubility and poor mutational robustness [25]. While these irregularities may not compromise the function of natural proteins in their natural context, they can severely hinder the process of repurposing them as therapeutic agents [28]. De novo proteins can overcome many of the limitations imposed by natural proteins because they can be designed to follow the principles of an idealized protein structure: regular secondary structure elements with short and structured connecting loops, well-packed hydrophobic cores, mostly polar exposed-surfaces, and highly satisfied polar
