In summary, de novo proteins can, by design, overcome many of the biochemical limitations inherent to natural proteins, thereby enabling the creation of potentially therapeutic proteins with highly desirable biochemical properties, such as high thermostability, manufacturability, solubility, and tunability.
## De novo design can be used to build potent proteins for immunotherapy
We recently described the first example of a de novo protein immunotherapeutic: Neo-2/15, a de novo mimic of the function of both interleukin-2 (IL-2) and interleukin-15 (IL-15) [35].
Recombinant interleukin-2 (rIL-2), also known as aldesleukin, was the first immunotherapy approved for the treatment of melanoma and renal cell carcinoma [9], but its clinical use has been limited due to severe doselimiting toxicities [11,74,75]. To accomplish its biological function, IL-2 induces the hetero-dimerization of two IL-2 cell-membrane receptors, namely the IL-2 beta $(\mathrm{IL-}2\mathrm{R}\upbeta)$ ) and the common-gamma $\langle\gamma_{\mathrm{c}}$ or IL$2\mathrm{R}\upgamma,$ ) receptors, also called the $\mathrm{IL-}\bar{2}\mathrm{R}\beta\gamma$ complex. The dimerization of $\mathrm{IL-}2\mathrm{R}\upbeta\upgamma$ initiates a signaling cascade that ultimately stimulates immune cells expressing the receptors, including those with anti-tumor function, such as effector $\mathrm{\DeltaT}$ cells and natural killer (NK) cells. However, many off-target cell types express a higheraffinity three-component IL-2-receptor containing in addition the IL-2 alpha-subunit $(\mathrm{IL}-2\mathrm{R}\alpha$ or CD25). Although CD25 is not directly involved in IL-2 signal transduction, its presence greatly enhances the affinity and activity of IL-2 (i.e., $\mathrm{IL}{-}2\mathrm{R}\alpha\upbeta\upgamma\ >>\ \mathrm{IL}{-}2\mathrm{R}\upbeta\upgamma)$ , meaning that CD25 is a potent allosteric activator of IL2. As a result, off-target $\mathrm{CD}25+$ cells, including endothelial cells and immunosuppressive regulatory T-cells, respond to high-dose IL-2 therapy with much higher potency than the intended target cells (e.g., $\mathrm{CD8+}$ naive T-cells). Such strong bias toward the activation of $\mathrm{CD}25+$ cells leads to a pleiotropic response with severe side effects [75,76].
Many efforts have been devoted to improving the safety and efficacy of IL-2 by means of eliminating/reducing its natural binding preference for CD25. Nevertheless, until recently, all efforts failed to completely abrogate CD25 binding while preserving strong $\mathrm{IL-}2\mathrm{R}\upbeta\upgamma$ binding. Furthermore, most of such reengineered IL-2 proteins have simultaneously introduced significant challenges to the potency, manufacturability, and/or stability of the resulting proteins [77e79]. To solve this decades-long challenge, we built on current Rosetta design methodology (extensively described in these two reviews [32,33]) to develop a state-of-the-art de novo computational and experimental protein design method that is capable of building fully de novo proteins that mimic the function of cytokines (and signaling proteins in general). We applied our method for building functional Neoleukin de novo protein mimics (www.neoleukin. com) with the aim of replicating the potent immunostimulatory function of both IL-2 and IL-15, while fully eliminating the binding site and dependency on the alpha receptor(s) (either CD25 or CD215). Our scientific efforts resulted in the creation of a novel and robust de novo computational design method and a number of fully de novo IL-2/IL-15 protein mimetics truly independent of CD25 and CD215. The best-optimized design, Neo-2/15 (Figure 2f), is a highly thermostable (Figure 3b) and tunable (Figure 3cee) protein that demonstrates potent signaling on both human and mouse cells expressing the $\mathrm{IL-}2\mathrm{R}\upbeta\upgamma$ receptors. Neo-2/ 15 exhibits both potent anti-tumor activity in syngeneic mouse tumor models and less toxicity than recombinant IL-2 [35]. Figure 4 shows new, independently conducted, in vivo studies confirming the therapeutic activity of Neo-2/15 in the murine CT26 model of colorectal cancer.
## Discussion, opportunities, and challenges
The exploration of novel protein functions through de novo design is enabling the development of new proteins with promising therapeutic applications [35,63]. In just two decades, computational protein engineering has advanced from just being able to optimize natural proteins to now creating entirely de novo proteins with extreme stability, a wide range of shapes, and custom functions. In contrast to current antibody-based therapies, de novo proteins can be small, thermally stable, easy to manufacture (usually in recombinant bacterial systems), and highly tunable. For the first time, we are now able to design custom proteins with a predefined functional purpose. The emerging understanding that we have acquired in creating de novo proteins is already allowing us to create a new class of custom-built therapeutic proteins with unique (and potentially superiorthan-natural) biochemical properties. The development of Neo-2/15 is a landmark d the first example of a de novo protein designed to solve a decades-long challenge in cancer immunotherapy [35].
De novo protein therapeutics still face at least two significant challenges. First, in order to create proteins with agonistic or antagonistic properties, the current computational methods rely on the availability of preexisting high-quality structural information of the targets. Further advancements in protein-structure prediction [80] and protein-structure determination [81,82] are poised to enhance the capabilities of de novo protein design. The second challenge is the potential for immunogenicity. It is well known that even recombinant human proteins can elicit a potent immunogenic response when administered to humans [26,28]. Therefore, it is reasonable to hypothesize that de novo proteins, with their low sequence homology to natural human proteins, could present a high immunogenic burden when dosed in immunocompetent patients. Nevertheless, two recent studies in murine models of de novo therapeutic proteins (one of them including Neo-2/15) suggest that this may not be the case. The studies showed that small (well-folded and hyperstable) de novo proteins elicit little (to no)
immunogenic response when administered intranasally, intraperitoneally, or intravenously into mice [35,63]. This unexpected lack of immunogenicity has been attributed to the unique biochemical and thermodynamic characteristics of the proteins, namely: its small size, extreme stability, and high solubility. Nevertheless, it is clear that further studies are required to understand the immunogenic risk that de novo proteins may pose. Future advancements in computational methods may be desirable to further reduce the risk of eliciting immunogenicity [83]. Nevertheless, paradoxically, an advantage that de novo protein therapeutics might offer is that an anti-drug antibody response against a de novo protein is unlikely to cross-react with any natural/endogenous human proteins [35], and therefore unlikely to lead to autoimmunity, which is a serious concern when adapting naturally-occurring human proteins (or variants thereof) for therapeutic purposes.
The de novo design methods that created Neo-2/15 can be applied to generate a wide range of novel therapeutic molecules with agonistic [35,84] or antagonistic activity [62,63,65] and sucessive iterations of this approach have the potential to enable more complex protein therapeutics, such as conditionally active signaling proteins (i.e., bioactive protein logic gates). We anticipate that future iterations of these methods will be a powerful driver for the development of next-generation therapeutics. It is certainly an exciting time in de novo protein design! With the emergence of the first examples of biologically relevant designed de novo proteins, it is clear that the field is coming of age.
